Entry: 5
Entry Type: Engineering Decision
Task Worked On: Implementation of evalPostFix in main.cpp
Decision/Issue: Wasn't sure to either choose integer division or double division using
the "/" operator
Alternative Approach: Integer division would give me 3 when dividing 7/2 rather than 3.5.
Double division would be better for accuracy.
Fix/Resolution: Used double division since it would give more accurate results. Using
ArrayStack<double> and stod(op) makes sure that the decimals are exact. If I used integer
division, like I said, dividing 7/2 would push 3 onto the stack rather than 3.5 which wouldn't
be as accurate. Since the order of popping matters too, b would be popped before a so that a/b could
give a correct answer. Double division would give us better answers would keep the correct decimals
throughout the operations done.
Commits: Used ArrayStack<double> and stod() for the use of double division.

Entry: 6
Entry type: Bug Fix
Task worked on: inFixToPostfix implemented into main.cpp
Issue: The while loop wasn't using the correct token to be used as the output vector
Error message: There wasn't an error message but the postfix wasn't giving the right output.
The token at the time was cloned instead of the the operator being taken from the stack.
What I tried: At first I put output.push_back(tokens[i]) since it was talking about the current
operator being used since tokens[i] was the operator that got triggered in the while loop.
Fix: Changed it to output.push_back({opStack.top()}) in order to have the operator on top of the
stack that is added to the output. This made the while loop drain higher precedence operators
off the stack in order to have opStack.top() be the right value to output.
Commits: Fixed token push in the while loop of infixToPostfix so I could use opStack.top() rather
than tokens[i].